================================================================================
RESEARCH ACKNOWLEDGMENTS & ADDITIONAL NOTICES
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Electrohome 1983 (WarGames Terminal Display Engine)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RetroTerm's WarGames display engine (80x24, 8x10 character matrix, P4 phosphor
CRT optical pipeline) uses an original clean-room C11 character generator and
renderer. No third-party font files, XPM bitmaps, PDFs, or film frames are
copied or redistributed.

Technical & Historical References Consulted:
- Jeffrey Swartz (CompuPro), "Making 'Wargames' computers compute required
  innovative programming", Mini-Micro Systems, June 1984 (pp. 135-145),
  https://bitsavers.org/magazines/Mini-Micro_Systems/198406_Making_Wargames.pdf
  (Primary hardware reference for the CompuPro System 816 / S-100 80x24 video
  system, replaceable character ROM, 48 Hz vertical sync to 24 fps cameras,
  and 17" Electrohome P4 monochrome CRT monitor.)
- Todd Fischer / Fischer-Freitas Co., "The WarGames IMSAI",
  https://www.imsai.net/the-wargames-imsai/
  (Historical production documentation on the IMSAI 8080 / IKB-1 prop setup
  and Electrohome 17" B&W composite monitor.)
- Michael Walden, "WarGames Terminal Fonts", https://mw.rat.bz/wgterm/
  (Consulted for technical analysis of the 8x10 logical character matrix,
  the 81 observed vs. 14 reconstructed printable ASCII glyph breakdown, and
  ASCII-1963 '^' (0x5E). His font files and XPM bitmaps under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0
  were NOT copied or redistributed.)
- Lord Nightmare, "WOPR Terminal",
  https://www.fontstruct.com/fontstructions/show/1616604/wopr-terminal
  (Consulted for technical analysis of the hardware character-ROM constraints:
  max 8 active vertical scanlines per glyph inside the 10-scanline cell,
  9th-pixel shift-register artifact, and full 10-scanline hardware block
  cursor. His FontStruct fonts under CC BY-SA 3.0 were NOT copied or
  redistributed.)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Digital Rain Screensaver (The Matrix, 1999)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RetroTerm's Matrix Digital Rain screensaver is based on primary frame-by-frame
research of the 1999 opening title sequence:
- Carl Newton, "The Matrix Digital Rain -- Frame-by-Frame Analysis",
  https://carlnewton.github.io/digital-rain-analysis/
  Repository: https://github.com/carlnewton/digital-rain (MIT License)
  (Primary source for the 53-glyph 1999 repertoire, viewBox 94x140 normalized
  into 16x24 coverage bitmaps in RetroTerm, and the six observed 1999
  animation rules: stationary grid, invisible prefixes, synchronized
  3-static + 1-cross-dissolve mutations, independent deletion strings,
  ~1-in-5 highlighted heads, and simultaneous cursor stammer.)
- Christopher L. Doyle (matrix.watch), "How the 1999 Matrix Digital Rain
  Actually Works", https://matrix.watch/articles/how-matrix-digital-rain-works
  (Consulted for comparative verification of 1999 vs. sequel behavior, glyph
  orientation cross-checks, and invisible-prefix / stammer context.)
- Historical Visual Design Credit: Original The Matrix (1999) digital rain
  visual design by Simon Whiteley and Animal Logic.

